**Simplest correct answer: `BlockingQueue` + an executor.** `ArrayBlockingQueue` is bounded: `put()` blocks when full and `take()` blocks when empty, so no manual locking is needed.

```java
BlockingQueue<Runnable> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10);
ExecutorService workers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
// Producer
Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
  for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    int id = i;
    try { q.put(() -> process(id)); }   // waits if full
    catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
  }
});
// Consumers
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
  workers.submit(() -> {
    try { while (true) q.take().run(); }  // processes in parallel
    catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
  });
}
```

**Hand-built version (what interviewers often want).** Use one lock with two conditions (`notFull`, `notEmpty`):

```java
class BoundedQueue<T> {
  private final Queue<T> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
  private final int cap;
  private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
  private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition(), notEmpty = lock.newCondition();
  BoundedQueue(int cap) { this.cap = cap; }
  void put(T t) throws InterruptedException {
    lock.lock();
    try { while (q.size() == cap) notFull.await();
          q.add(t); notEmpty.signal(); } finally { lock.unlock(); }
  }
  T take() throws InterruptedException {
    lock.lock();
    try { while (q.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();
          T t = q.remove(); notFull.signal(); return t; } finally { lock.unlock(); }
  }
}
```

**Key points.** Use `while` (not `if`) around `await()`/`wait()` to handle spurious wakeups; the lock guards the shared state; signalling after each change wakes the opposite side. With several consumers, tasks run in parallel as each `take()` removes a different task.

**Shutdown.** Use a poison pill (a special sentinel task, one per consumer), or interrupt the workers and call `shutdown()`/`awaitTermination()`. Handle exceptions inside tasks so a failure does not kill a worker. Choose the bound to apply backpressure and protect memory.
